{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789827183,"nonce":"n9gd/09nz4f1+4v8IMXCVK4u/kk=","message":"d48b546: the executables export only the plugin ABI (wren*, wlift_*, wrenlift_*), through build.rs and tools/link/exports.*; the blanket -export_dynamic kept every LLVM backend alive. Stripped wlift with static LLVM: 184 MB → 78 MB (31 MB gzipped) on macOS arm64; Ash's Linux binary stripped is 147 MB (44 MB gzipped). Plugins (hatch sweep 42/42) and the e2e self-dlsym tests pass on macOS and the NUC.\n\n1bac040: LLVM is a rung above the Cranelift optimised tier rather than the optimised tier itself. Cause of the site regression at 55f71b6 (method_call 0.017→0.043, delta_blue 0.056→0.075): every optimised compile went to LLVM, 50–200 ms each on a worker, and the site programs end before that. Now the Cranelift body lands first; after WLIFT_LLVM_AFTER_MS (250) a timer raises each Cranelift-opt body's retier_top word, the body reports in at entry or an outermost header, the LLVM compile is queued, and the transfer goes through wren_retier with the caller's generation in the header word. Two things found on the way: (1) field-kind notes on SetField were emitted in every Cranelift body when LLVM was on, costing 27% on bench/nbody's Cranelift-opt body; baseline only now. (2) an LLVM result compiled with speculation while the Cranelift body's guard failed was installed and deopted on every call (delta_blue 5k deopts); such results are dropped. (3) LLVM ignored ColdLoopExit, so dyn_dispatch's loop stayed generic under LLVM (2.7× slower); it counts now.\n\nNUC (idle, 6-run medians, s): cranelift-only / ladder 250 ms / LLVM at 0 ms — ash/nbody 2.34 / 1.27 / 1.15; ash/mandelbrot 0.53 / 0.49 / 0.45; ash/method_call 0.122 / 0.133 / 0.082; ash/closure_call 0.165 / 0.179 / 0.082; bench/nbody 0.130 / 0.129 / 0.099; bench/delta_blue 0.064 / 0.067 / 0.129 (before the stale-result fix). The ~8% the ladder costs on a single tight outer loop (method_call, closure_call) is the header poll; LLVM at 0 ms would win those by 35% once its early-compile losses are gone, so the wait can come down when delta_blue under early LLVM is understood.","files":null}]}